From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: glacier <hyouga.dev@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 3 Nov 2026 21:07:56 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] Keep shaped blocks' bounds per thread, and fix logins, kicks,
 stops and unloads around world threads

What a review of the world-thread patches found:

- Blocks are one object for every world, and the shaped ones (stairs, slabs,
  fences, doors, trapdoors, ladders, walls, ...) write their bounds and read
  them back. Bounds set after registration now go to a copy for the calling
  thread (Block.bounds). The furnace swap flag, a redstone wire's update set
  and power flag and the stair ray trace part are per thread
  (AthenaThreadState); the torch toggles map is synchronized and the potion
  effect cache concurrent.
- A second login of a player in a world on its own thread kicks the old one
  on that thread; the new login waits in LoginListener's state e.
- A stop that times out no longer leaves the world with a thread that is
  gone: the thread hands the world back when its tick ends (athenaTakeBack).
- A kick from another thread goes to the player's world's thread; if that
  thread is stuck, the server thread does the player list's half of the
  disconnect and writes the kick at once (PlayerList.athenaDetach), and so
  does a second login. A login is not handed over into a stuck world.
- A player on its way into a world being unloaded or halted lands at the
  main world's spawn; unloadWorld keeps a world that a player got into.
- A player handed to another thread has its position and owner moved first,
  its packets wait until it lands (athenaRunWhenLanded), and teleportAsync
  completes then. A player that quit on the way arrives nowhere.
- At shutdown a world whose thread does not stop is not saved. World threads
  are counted from start(), and a finished chunk load queued for a world
  just handed back follows it.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

